# =============================================================================
# CTIParsor configuration
# =============================================================================
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   Copy it to .env (setup.sh does this for you) and edit .env, never this file.
#   Docker: every service reads .env; apply a change with  docker compose up -d
#   Host install: `python run_api.py` reads .env when it starts.
#   A variable already set in the process environment wins over .env (under
#   systemd, an `Environment=` line in the unit beats this file).
#
# CONVENTIONS
#   KEY=value     a setting to review
#   # KEY=value   optional: the value shown is the default used when unset
#   Booleans take 1/true/yes/on or 0/false/no/off.  Anything else falls back
#   to the default, so a typo never switches a feature on.
#   Numbers work the same way: a value that does not parse falls back to the
#   default, with a warning in the log.
#   One KEY=value per line, no quotes, no comment after the value.
#
# MINIMUM TO GET RUNNING
#   1. LLM_PROVIDER, plus that provider's key or URL and model   (section 1)
#      Left as shipped, LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic has no key, so Stage 3 is skipped.
#      With STIX_TLP=amber (the default) a hosted provider is refused even with
#      a key: see section 1 and docs/configuration.md#egress-policy-tlp.
#   2. Docker only: CTI_DB_PASSWORD, generated by setup.sh        (section 11)
#   Everything else has a working default.
#
# SECTIONS
#    1. LLM: provider, model, key (Stage 3 extraction)
#    2. LLM: extra checking passes (Stages 3d, 3e, 3f, document-level)
#    3. LLM: throughput and request limits
#    4. Vision: figure reading (Stage 1f)
#    5. NLP models (Stages 2c, 2d, 2e)
#    6. Analyst feedback: calibrated thresholds and overrides
#    7. CVE enrichment (Stage 2f)
#    8. STIX output
#    9. Web server: API and UI
#   10. Worker and job queue
#   11. Database: PostgreSQL
#   12. Docker Compose: ports, image, resource limits
#   13. Logging
#   14. Expert tuning: change only alongside a measurement
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1. LLM: provider, model, key (Stage 3 extraction)
# =============================================================================
# Stage 3 reads each chunk of the report and extracts actors, malware, tools,
# techniques and the relationships between them.  Pick ONE provider; only its
# block below is used.  Without a usable provider Stage 3 is skipped and the
# bundle holds only what Stages 1-2 found (IoCs, gazetteer, NER).
#
#   anthropic   hosted, needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (default)
#   gemini      hosted, needs GEMINI_API_KEY
#   mistral     hosted, needs MISTRAL_API_KEY
#   ollama      self-hosted
#   lmstudio    self-hosted
#   vllm        self-hosted
#
# Self-hosted server + Docker: `localhost` inside a container is the container
# itself.  Use http://host.docker.internal:<port> for a server on this machine,
# or the server's LAN address.
LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic

# TLP egress policy (ADR-0097): an AMBER, AMBER+STRICT or RED report (an unmarked
# one is AMBER unless STIX_TLP says otherwise) goes only to a local provider: vllm,
# ollama or lmstudio whose base URL host is a loopback or private address, or a
# name listed here (comma-separated, case-insensitive; this replaces the default).
# No DNS lookup is made: a hostname that is not listed is external.
# LLM_LOCAL_HOSTS=localhost,ollama,host.docker.internal

# Seconds one LLM request may take before its chunk is skipped.  120 suits the
# hosted APIs.  A local model needs more: Qwen3.8-27B on vLLM (~24 tokens/s)
# took 79 s for the extraction call on a single 1000-character chunk.
# For a local Ollama, around 600 is a starting point.
LLM_TIMEOUT=120

# --- Anthropic  (key: https://console.anthropic.com/) ------------------------
# Empty: Stage 3 is skipped.  A value that is not a real key makes it run
# and fail on every report.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5

# --- Google Gemini  (key: https://aistudio.google.com/app/apikey) ------------
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-pro

# --- Mistral AI  (key: https://console.mistral.ai/) --------------------------
# Models: mistral-small-latest | mistral-medium-latest | mistral-large-latest
MISTRAL_API_KEY=
MISTRAL_MODEL=mistral-small-latest

# --- Ollama  (https://ollama.com; run `ollama pull <model>` first) -----------
# For CTI extraction, best to fastest:
#   mistral-nemo  ~7 GB   best JSON adherence, handles long reports
#   mistral       ~4 GB   good quality for its size
#   llama3.2      ~2 GB   fastest, adequate for short reports
# Keep OLLAMA_MODEL set: when unset the code falls back to llama3.2, the
# weakest of the three at structured output.  The value below is mistral as
# shipped, not that fallback: set the model you pulled.
# The compose `ollama` profile runs one for you: OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434
# The code uses Ollama's OpenAI-compatible endpoint, which takes no num_ctx,
# so the context window is the server's: CTI_OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH for the
# compose `ollama` profile, OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH on your own Ollama server.
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
OLLAMA_MODEL=mistral
# Only if the server was started with a key; the key is sent as a Bearer token.
# OLLAMA_API_KEY=

# --- LM Studio  (local server, default port 1234) ----------------------------
LMSTUDIO_BASE_URL=http://localhost:1234
LMSTUDIO_MODEL=lmstudio-model
# Only if the server was started with a key; the key is sent as a Bearer token.
# LMSTUDIO_API_KEY=

# --- vLLM  (default port 8000) -----------------------------------------------
# VLLM_MODEL must match the id the server serves EXACTLY, organisation prefix
# included (e.g. Inferact/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4).  List the served ids with
#   curl -s <VLLM_BASE_URL>/v1/models
# A wrong name gets a 404 on every call; Stage 3 is then recorded as failed in
# the job's run config (stage_report) and its results are empty (ADR-0060).
VLLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
VLLM_MODEL=vllm-model
# Qwen3-family models "think" before answering unless told not to.  Measured
# on Qwen3.8-27B: the thinking used the whole 8192-token output budget on the
# Stage 3 prompt (431 s) and returned no JSON.  false sends
# chat_template_kwargs.enable_thinking=false with every vLLM request, text and
# figures alike; a chat template without that flag ignores it.
VLLM_ENABLE_THINKING=false
# Only if the server was started with a key, e.g. `vllm serve --api-key`; the key
# is sent as a Bearer token.
# VLLM_API_KEY=

# --- Sampling (every provider) ---------------------------------------------
# Unset: each provider's own default (vLLM: the model's generation config,
# e.g. temperature 0.7 for Qwen; Anthropic: 1.0), so two runs of one report can
# differ.  Set them to make runs repeatable, e.g. for an evaluation
# (docs/eval/README.md).  The seed is sent to vLLM, Ollama and LM Studio only.
# LLM_TEMPERATURE=0
# LLM_SEED=13


# =============================================================================
# 2. LLM: extra checking passes (each one adds LLM calls)
# =============================================================================
# Stage 3d, relationship verification: a second call must quote the sentence
# that supports each extracted relationship, and unsupported ones are dropped
# (hallucinated relationships went from ~27% to ~8% in the aCTIon paper).
# Costs ~1.4x the LLM calls.
ENABLE_STIX_VERIFICATION=true
# Skip the check on chunks with fewer relationships than this.
STIX_VERIFY_MIN_RELS=1
# Claims per verification call.  Every answer carries a quote, so the ~100
# claims of a document-level pass in one call would outgrow the output budget,
# and a cut-off answer keeps all of them unverified (ADR-0064).
# STIX_VERIFY_BATCH_SIZE=40

# Stage 3f, technique (TTP) verification: the same check for techniques.
# Techniques already backed by a high-confidence semantic match (Stage 2c) are
# trusted and skipped, so the cost also stays near 1.4x.  Recommended on:
# without it techniques get no evidence check at all, and on a 12-page report
# with no explicit T-IDs they were the largest source of unsupported claims.
ENABLE_TTP_VERIFICATION=true
# Skip the check on chunks with fewer single-signal techniques than this.
TTP_VERIFY_MIN=1

# TTP mode (ADR-0072).  "select", the default since the 2026-10-03 dev run
# (docs/eval/baseline-2026-10.md: technique F1 0.607 against 0.465): 2c only
# retrieves candidates (ATT&CK descriptions and procedure examples, BM25 and
# dense fused) and 3f chooses among them and the LLM's own proposals, each
# with a quote the code finds in the text; a failed selection ships nothing
# (its candidates go to review).  Retrieval reads the committed corpus
# (pipeline/data/attack_retrieval_*); after an ATT&CK release:
#   python scripts/build_indexes.py --only retrieval
# "verify": 2c emits techniques from cosine similarity and 3f checks the LLM's.
# TTP_MODE=select

# ATT&CK release (ADR-0087).  Unset = the track shipped in pipeline/data/
# (19.2, named in its attack_manifest.json).  "20.0" reads the files of
# pipeline/data/attack/20.0/ instead: the technique index, gazetteer, revoked
# ids, curated relationships and both embedding sets.  An unknown value stops
# the run before it starts, never falls back to another release.  Read once at
# process start, like TTP_EMBEDDING_MODEL: restart the API or the CLI after
# changing it.  The run manifest records the release and the file hashes.
# ATTACK_RELEASE=20.0

# Stage 3e, cross-model consensus: chunks with relationships also go through a
# SECOND provider.  Relationships both models find gain confidence; the others
# lose it and cannot auto-promote in review.  Stronger than 3d (two models
# instead of one checking itself) but doubles the LLM cost of those chunks.
# CONSENSUS_PROVIDER must differ from LLM_PROVIDER and be configured in
# section 1 (key or URL).  The mistral below is as shipped: it has no effect
# while ENABLE_CONSENSUS=false, and the code's default is empty.
ENABLE_CONSENSUS=false
CONSENSUS_PROVIDER=mistral

# Document-level relation pass (ADR-0057, ADR-0064): one extra call per report
# reads the WHOLE document and extracts relationships only, linking facts
# stated far apart ("X is a variant of Y" in paragraph 2, "Y is attributed to
# Z" in paragraph 40).  Measured on 7 real reports (ADR-0064): about 60 % more
# correct relationships, but only 40 % of the ones it adds are strictly correct
# (vague related-to, generic endpoints).  Needs a context window that fits the
# whole report (LLM_DOC_MAX_PROMPT_LENGTH, section 3) and room for a long
# answer: on a 37 000-character report it wrote 50 000 characters, which takes
# 11 minutes at ~15 tokens/s.  Raise LLM_TIMEOUT and LLM_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS
# (section 3) accordingly, or the call times out and adds nothing.
ENABLE_DOCUMENT_LEVEL_RELATIONS=true


# =============================================================================
# 3. LLM: throughput and request limits
# =============================================================================
# Chunks sent to the LLM at the same time.  Raise it to go faster on a hosted
# API; lower it if the provider answers 429 (rate limit).
# Ollama: 1.  Requests beyond the server's parallel slots wait in its queue,
# and that wait counts against LLM_TIMEOUT.
LLM_PARALLELISM=3
# Save a checkpoint every N finished chunks, so an interrupted report resumes.
# A checkpoint is only resumed by the same document, model and prompts (ADR-0059).
CHECKPOINT_EVERY=5

# Size limits.  Keep LLM_MAX_RESPONSE_LENGTH above ~4 x LLM_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS:
# a response cut shorter than the model may write loses its JSON, and the
# chunk with it.
# LLM_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192
# LLM_MAX_PROMPT_LENGTH=32000
# LLM_MAX_RESPONSE_LENGTH=48000
# Prompt ceiling for the document-level pass only: it must fit a whole report
# (the largest in this project's corpus is ~110k characters).
# LLM_DOC_MAX_PROMPT_LENGTH=300000
# Ollama cuts a prompt that is longer than its context window, with no error.
# The window is OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH on your own server, and
# CTI_OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH for the compose `ollama` profile (16384 tokens by
# default in compose.yaml).  A whole report of 300000 characters is far beyond
# it: with Ollama, raise the context or lower LLM_DOC_MAX_PROMPT_LENGTH.

# NO EFFECT at the moment.  The worker picks the chunk size itself (3000
# characters, 4000 above 30k, 5000 above 60k) with a 400-character overlap, and
# Stage 3 retries a failed call 3 times.  These three are read into a config
# object that nothing uses; setting them changes nothing.
# CHUNK_MAX_CHARS=3000
# CHUNK_OVERLAP=400
# LLM_MAX_RETRIES=3


# =============================================================================
# 4. Vision: figure reading (Stage 1f, PDF only; ADR-0032, ADR-0033, ADR-0094)
# =============================================================================
# Reads the figures of a PDF (screenshots, diagrams, attack chains) and adds
# their text and arrows to the report before extraction.  On by default: the
# default, auto, reads them with the provider chosen for the text
# (LLM_PROVIDER, section 1), with the model it would use if VISION_PROVIDER
# named it.  A figure never goes to another provider than the one chosen for
# the text: an install that moves the text to a local LLM keeps its figures
# local.  Most text models cannot see (and a text model handed an image
# invents rather than errors), so before any figure is sent the model is probed
# for image support; when it cannot see, or no key or server answers, Stage 1f
# is skipped (logged at INFO, not WARNING, and the reason in the stage report),
# as Stage 3 is without a provider.  The server URL and the key come from
# section 1.
#
#   auto        the provider of LLM_PROVIDER, when it is one of the four below
#               (default).  gemini and lmstudio have no figure reader: skipped
#   none        figures are not read
#   anthropic   default model claude-haiku-5-5
#   ollama      default model qwen3.8; needs a model with the `vision`
#               capability (curl -s <OLLAMA_BASE_URL>/api/tags)
#   mistral     VISION_MODEL required: no vision model name was verified
#   vllm        default model VLLM_MODEL on VLLM_BASE_URL; the server must run
#               a multimodal model
#
# A named provider (anything but auto) overrides LLM_PROVIDER, and warns when
# it cannot serve instead of logging at INFO.
#
# Figures go where the text goes: under auto, a run with Stage 3 disabled
# (main.py --no-llm, evaluation --disable 3, or 3 in PIPELINE_DISABLED_STAGES)
# sends no text to any LLM and reads no figure either (the stage report says
# so).  A named provider is an explicit choice and still reads them.
#
# Cost: up to 40 figure reads per PDF (pipeline/stage1f_figures.py), billed on
# anthropic.  On a local model, a read took about 40 s on one GPU (ADR-0032):
# a 40-figure report can spend tens of minutes in Stage 1f.  Setting
# VISION_PROVIDER to none turns Stage 1f off.  An install whose .env
# already says VISION_PROVIDER=none keeps figures off until it says auto.
VISION_PROVIDER=auto
# Empty = the provider's default model above.
VISION_MODEL=
# Seconds allowed per figure.
VISION_TIMEOUT_S=120

# Figures read at the same time, for ollama and vllm (anthropic and mistral
# always use 4).  1 was measured on Ollama with one GPU: 4 in flight made each
# call ~3x slower and total throughput worse.  Not yet measured on vLLM.
# VISION_CONCURRENCY=1

# Skip the image-support probe.  Only for a provider whose capabilities cannot
# be read (Mistral), with a model you KNOW can see; it logs a warning each time.
# Leave it unset for ollama and vllm: their probe also catches a wrong model
# name.  true skips the probe.
# VISION_ASSUME_CAPABLE=false


# =============================================================================
# 5. NLP models (Stages 2c, 2d, 2e; run locally, no LLM involved)
# =============================================================================
# Stage 2c, semantic technique matching (sentence embeddings -> ATT&CK):
#   all-MiniLM-L6-v2              80 MB, general English (default)
#   ehsanaghaei/SecureBERT-Plus   500 MB, security vocabulary, better recall on
#                                 jargon (+8-12% F1 in the CTiKG paper)
# After a change, rebuild the cache:
#   python scripts/build_indexes.py --only embeddings
TTP_EMBEDDING_MODEL=all-MiniLM-L6-v2

# Stage 2d, CyNER cybersecurity entity recognition.  Off by default (ADR-0099):
# measured end to end with the LLM, the pipeline without it has half the false
# malware and threat actors for almost the same recall.  true runs the stage:
# it adds about 2 GB of memory and a few seconds of CPU per report.  The
# bootstrap downloads its model only when this is true: turned on later, the
# model is downloaded at the first report, or the stage is skipped offline.
# CYNER_ENABLED=false

# Stage 2 and 2d, precision filters (ADR-0088, docs/eval/ner-precision-2026-10.md):
# a CyNER threat actor written as ordinary language ("cybercriminals",
# "Chinese threat actors", "Iran") is dropped, and a spaCy location is kept
# only when it can be a place name ("C&C", "XSS" go; "the United States"
# becomes "United States").  false = the behaviour before the filters.
NER_PRECISION_FILTERS=true

# Stage 2b, the gazetteer's ordinary-word policy (docs/gazetteer-ordinary-words.md:
# "page", "play", "route" are MITRE aliases and words).  shadow = match as
# before and count, in the stage report, the occurrences the policy would
# withhold; apply = withhold them (for measurement, until the document's
# acceptance gates pass: python scripts/measure_gazetteer_policy.py);
# off = no policy.
# GAZETTEER_POLICY=shadow
# CYNER_MODEL=PranavaKailash/CyNER-2.0-DeBERTa-v3-base

# Stage 2e, GLiNER zero-shot entity recognition, for what the others miss:
# targeted sectors, campaigns, infrastructure, actors and malware not yet in
# ATT&CK.  It asks for the entity types GLINER_ENTITY_TYPES, below, names.
#   urchade/gliner_large-v2.1    ~1.8 GB   most accurate, slowest (default)
#   urchade/gliner_medium-v2.1   ~0.8 GB   balanced, CPU-friendly
#   urchade/gliner_small-v2.1    ~0.6 GB   fastest, lower recall
# numind/NuNER-Zero and NuNER-Zero-span were removed from Hugging Face.
GLINER_MODEL=urchade/gliner_large-v2.1
# Minimum score to keep an entity (calibrated rows, section 6, override it).
GLINER_THRESHOLD=0.40
# false skips Stage 2e and never loads its model.
GLINER_ENABLED=true
# The entity types Stage 2e asks its model for, separated by commas, among:
#   identity (targeted sectors), location (targeted countries), campaign,
#   infrastructure, malware, threat_actor
# Default: identity,location (ADR-0098).  Empty: the stage is skipped and its
# model is not loaded.  In 0.7.0 the stage returned no entity, so the bundles
# now carry the sectors and countries it finds.  Measured without the LLM on
# AnnoCTR dev: sectors 37 correct for 30 false, countries 7 for 4; but threat
# actors 3 for 51 and malware 1 for 17, and campaigns 8 for 58 on the in-house
# set.  Not measured together with the LLM.  The model is most of a report's
# memory (docs/deployment.md).
GLINER_ENTITY_TYPES=identity,location

# Hugging Face token, used only when a model is downloaded (bootstrap or first
# run).  Optional: without one, the clean install of TESTING.md downloaded
# the 2.6 GB of default models in under 2 minutes (2026-11-01).
# A token (free at https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens) raises Hugging
# Face's rate limits for your account.
HF_TOKEN=


# =============================================================================
# 6. Analyst feedback: calibrated thresholds and overrides
# =============================================================================
# ADR-0051: per-entity-type cutoffs learned from the analysts' accept/reject
# decisions override GLINER_THRESHOLD and CyNER's 0.70.  Written by
#   python -m scripts.calibrate_thresholds --apply
# or POST /api/thresholds/recalibrate.  false = ignore them, use the defaults.
THRESHOLD_CALIBRATION_ENABLED=true

# ADR-0052: an active `deny` row drops that (value, type) from every NER stage;
# an active `promote` row adds the name to the Stage 2b gazetteer.  Rows are
# proposed by  python -m scripts.promote_overrides --apply  (or
# POST /api/overrides/promote) and activated by a person
# (PATCH /api/overrides/{id}).  false = ignore every row.
ENTITY_OVERRIDES_ENABLED=true

# ADR-0090: enriching a STIX 2.1 bundle the analyst already has.  On by default.
# false turns it off: while false, every request that carries a baseline bundle,
# and /api/baselines/inspect, answers 400.
STIX_ENRICHMENT_ENABLED=true

# ADR-0058: when a job finishes, the worker auto-accepts entities at >= 90 %
# confidence, but leaves this share of them pending (flagged "confirm" in the
# review page).  Analysts' verdicts on those rows measure how often
# auto-accept is right (GET /api/thresholds -> auto_accept_audit).  0 disables.
# REVIEW_CONTROL_SAMPLE_RATE=0.10


# =============================================================================
# 7. CVE enrichment (Stage 2f; calls the internet, off by default)
# =============================================================================
# Looks each CVE up on the public CIRCL API to add a description and a CVSS v3
# score to its STIX Vulnerability.  Off by default because Stages 1, 2, 4 and 5
# otherwise run fully offline.  Results, misses included, are cached in the
# database (table cve_cache).  At most 25 lookups and 30 s per report.
# true turns it on.
# CVE_ENRICHMENT=false

# A value the report names without accusing it (a reference link, a popular
# domain, a private address) ships without an Indicator (docs/configuration.md).
# BENIGN_IOC_FILTER=true


# =============================================================================
# 8. STIX output
# =============================================================================
# TLP marking stamped on every object when a report was imported without one:
# clear | green | amber | amber+strict | red (a TLP: prefix and WHITE are
# accepted).  CLEAR, GREEN, AMBER and RED are the standard marking ids OpenCTI
# and MISP recognise; AMBER+STRICT is emitted as OpenCTI defines it (ADR-0073).
# Anything else stops the API, the worker and the CLI at startup: a marking
# is never guessed.  The default is AMBER; say `clear` if that is what you
# share.  A TLP chosen for a report at import wins over this default.
STIX_TLP=amber
# The Identity every object names as its author (created_by_ref): the tool
# that produced the bundle, not the threat actor.
STIX_AUTHOR_NAME=CTIParsor


# =============================================================================
# 9. Web server: API and UI
# =============================================================================
# One process serves the API (/api/*) and the UI (/).  These are read by
# `python run_api.py`, not by a bare `uvicorn` command.  Under Docker, compose
# forces API_HOST=0.0.0.0, API_PORT=8000 and API_RELOAD=0 inside the container:
# publish with CTI_BIND / CTI_PORT (section 12) instead.
#
# 127.0.0.1 = this machine only.  0.0.0.0 = the whole network: with AUTH_MODE=off
# the API refuses to start on it, because anyone who reaches the port could read,
# edit and delete every report, change the pipeline policy and make the server
# fetch URLs.  See AUTH_MODE below and docs/deployment.md.
# API_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Authentication (ADR-0097; docs/deployment.md, "Authentication").
#   off   = none (the default); allowed only with API_HOST on loopback.
#   local = local accounts, roles, sessions and API tokens.  Create the first
#           admin with `python -m api.admin create-admin --username NAME`.
#   oidc  = sign-in with an OpenID Connect identity provider (OIDC_* below).
#   both  = local accounts and OIDC.
# Under Docker the address judged is CTI_BIND (section 12), not API_HOST: the default
# stack starts with off; a CTI_BIND beyond loopback needs local, oidc or both.
# AUTH_MODE=off
# Lifetime of a web session in hours; each request slides it.
# SESSION_TTL_HOURS=12
# Sign-in attempts per client address (slowapi syntax).  Five attempts for one username
# lock it for 15 minutes, its owner included; `python -m api.admin set-password` unlocks.
# LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT=10/minute
# OIDC (AUTH_MODE=oidc or both; docs/deployment.md).  The API refuses to start without
# the first five, or with a non-https issuer or redirect URI.
# OIDC_ISSUER_URL=https://idp.example.org/realms/cti
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=ctiparsor
# A file holding the client secret, read at each use (no secret in an env var).
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/oidc_client_secret
# The full callback URL registered at the provider (never guessed from the Host header).
# OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=https://cti.example.org/api/auth/oidc/callback
# group=role pairs; the highest mapped role wins; no mapped group, no sign-in.
# OIDC_ROLE_MAP=cti-admins=admin,cti-analysts=analyst,cti-readers=reader
# OIDC_SCOPES: space-separated, default "openid profile email" (openid is required)
# OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM=groups
# OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM=preferred_username
# Days a row of the audit table (who changed what, docs/operations.md, "Audit trail") is kept.
# Older rows are deleted, at most once an hour.  0 keeps every row.
# AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# API_PORT=8000

# The Host names the API answers to (comma-separated, port ignored).  Any
# other Host gets 400 before a handler runs: that is what stops a hostile web
# page from pointing its own name at 127.0.0.1 and reading every report as
# same-origin (DNS rebinding) in an app with no authentication.  The same
# list decides which Origin a browser may write from; cross-site writes get
# 403.  List the name or address analysts reach the app on when it is not
# loopback (the LAN address behind API_HOST=0.0.0.0, the public name behind
# the proxy profile).  `*.example.org` works; `*` turns the check off.
# API_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1,[::1]

# uvicorn processes.  Keep 1 unless you measured memory: the per-process job
# limit (WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENT) and the model memory both multiply by it.
# API_WORKERS=1

# Restart when a source file changes.  Development only; costs memory.
# API_RELOAD=false

# Peers trusted to set X-Forwarded-For / X-Forwarded-Proto.  Keep the default
# for a direct connection or an nginx on the same host (docs/deployment.md,
# Option C).  Set "*" with the compose `proxy` profile: nginx is then a
# separate container, and with the default every analyst would share one
# per-IP upload quota (10/minute).  "*" is safe there because the app's own
# port is not published.
# FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS=127.0.0.1
#
# URL capture (ADR-0029): the URL tab renders the page in Chromium.
#
# Its sandbox needs unprivileged user namespaces.  Set 1, true or yes to render
# without it, for a host or container runtime that cannot grant them: each
# capture then logs a warning, and a renderer exploit on a captured page runs
# code on this host.  `on` does not count here.  Unset = the sandbox stays on.
# CTIPARSOR_CAPTURE_UNSANDBOXED=false
#
# The capture's traffic goes through this proxy, and a URL capture is refused
# without it (HTTP 503).  compose sets http://capture-proxy:3128, a Squid egress
# filter that lets the capture reach public addresses only and resolves and
# connects in one step, which closes the DNS-rebinding gap for every request,
# redirect hops included.  Without the proxy, the Playwright driver resolves the
# requests itself and ignores the Chromium DNS pin.
# CTIPARSOR_CAPTURE_PROXY=
# Development and tests only: capture without the proxy.  The DNS-rebinding gap
# is then open, and each capture logs a warning saying so.
# CTIPARSOR_CAPTURE_ALLOW_DIRECT=false


# =============================================================================
# 10. Worker and job queue
# =============================================================================
# Each report runs in its own subprocess and loads the NLP models: memory is
# the limit, not CPU.
#
# Reports processed at the same time.  Each holds ~4.4 GB (mostly GLiNER).  The
# default is 1, for a host install as for the compose worker.  Raise it only on
# a host with the RAM for it: floor((RAM_GB - 4) / 4.4), or the value that
# scripts/measure_cold_start.py suggests (docs/deployment.md, section 5).  In
# the compose stack, raise CTI_WORKER_MEMORY by 4.4 GB per extra report.
# WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENT=1

# Seconds one report may run (figures and LLM included) before it is cancelled.
# A local LLM needs more: one short chunk took ~100 s with Stages 3d and 3f
# run on Qwen3.8-27B served by vLLM.
# WORKER_JOB_TIMEOUT=1800

# Reports allowed to wait once every slot is busy; beyond it an upload gets
# HTTP 503 instead of being silently dropped.  0 = unbounded.
# API_QUEUE_MAX_DEPTH=50

# Delete finished jobs (database rows and files) older than N days.  0 = never:
# a job only goes away when someone deletes it.  Queued and running jobs are
# never swept.  The sweep runs at most every JOB_RETENTION_SWEEP_S seconds.
# JOB_RETENTION_DAYS=0
# JOB_RETENTION_SWEEP_S=3600
#
# Where the files are kept.  Empty = the default: the folder of that name at the
# project root.  uploads/ holds uploaded sources and URL-capture metadata,
# output/ exported bundles and Stage 3 checkpoints, state/ what the app keeps
# between starts (db-identity.json, ADR-0077).  The image keeps uploads/ and
# output/ as links into the cti-state volume, which is /app/state.
# CTIPARSOR_UPLOADS_DIR=
# CTIPARSOR_OUTPUT_DIR=
# CTIPARSOR_STATE_DIR=

# Who runs the pipeline (ADR-0046): all = this process (host install); api =
# accept and queue reports only; worker = run queued reports.  Compose sets
# api / worker on its services.
# CTIPARSOR_ROLE=all
# A running job whose heartbeat is older than the lease goes back to the
# queue.  Keep the lease at least 3x the heartbeat.
# WORKER_HEARTBEAT_S=30
# WORKER_LEASE_TIMEOUT_S=180
# Queue poll interval, and how long a stopping worker lets running reports
# finish before requeueing them.
# WORKER_POLL_S=2
# WORKER_DRAIN_S=60
# A job whose lease expired is requeued while it has been claimed fewer times
# than this (each claim counts one attempt); then it is marked failed with the
# reason "quarantined after N attempts", not requeued (ADR-0097 item 20).
# JOB_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# Uploads and pasted text are refused with HTTP 507 while the disk that holds
# uploads/ has less free space than this, in MB.  0 turns the check off.
# UPLOAD_MIN_FREE_MB=1024
# The file the worker touches on every poll.  The compose healthcheck reads the
# same variable, and fails once the file is two minutes old.  It must be
# writable: the container's /tmp is a tmpfs.
# WORKER_ALIVE_FILE=/tmp/ctiparsor-worker.alive

# Corpus sync (tarball corpora): a download above this many bytes is refused, and
# the partial file removed, as is an archive whose members add up to more than
# CORPUS_EXTRACT_MAX_BYTES once extracted (#614).  Both in bytes.
# CORPUS_TARBALL_MAX_BYTES=2147483648
# CORPUS_EXTRACT_MAX_BYTES=8589934592

# Tesseract language(s) for scanned PDF pages, e.g. eng+fra+ukr+pol+rus (the
# image installs fra, ukr, pol and rus; another language needs its
# tesseract-ocr-<lang> package).  OCR is decided page by page.  A language
# without data is left out with a warning.  The default is English alone.
# OCR_LANG=eng

# ADR-0059: stages to skip for every report this process runs (API worker and
# CLI alike), comma-separated ids from: 1f 2 2b 2c 2d 2e 2g 3 3d 3f 3e 3doc 2f
# 4 4b 4c 5.  1 (ingestion) and 3r (baseline reconciliation, which Stage 4's
# merge of an enrich job's baseline needs) cannot be disabled: naming one fails
# the run.
# Meant for measuring what a stage contributes; each job's run config records
# which stages ran (stage_report).
# PIPELINE_DISABLED_STAGES=


# =============================================================================
# 11. Database: PostgreSQL (required)
# =============================================================================
# Holds everything: jobs, entities, relationships, the policy, the figure and
# CVE caches, and the detection-rule corpus (ADR-0045, ADR-0053; SQLite is no
# longer supported).
#
# Docker: compose builds the connection from the three values below.
# CTI_DB_PASSWORD is required; setup.sh generates it and copies it to
# .secrets/db_password, so re-run  bash setup.sh  after changing it.  Changing
# the user or password once the pg-data volume exists does not change the
# server: alter the role in psql, or start from a fresh volume.
# CTI_DB_USER=ctiparsor
# CTI_DB_NAME=ctiparsor
CTI_DB_PASSWORD=

# Host install (python run_api.py, pytest): required, or they stop with an
# error; main.py, the batch CLI, does not use the database.  The password goes
# in PGPASSWORD rather than in the URL, so it needs no escaping and never
# appears in a logged connection string.  DATABASE_URL has no default: the
# value below is an example.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://ctiparsor@localhost:5432/ctiparsor
# PGPASSWORD=
#
# Every start (API, worker, bootstrap) brings the schema up to date under one
# lock (ADR-0077).  A process that finds another one migrating waits this many
# seconds, then stops with an error.  Raise it before a long data migration.
# DB_MIGRATION_LOCK_TIMEOUT_S=600
#
# Upgrading from the old single-SQLite-file layout: run once, in this order,
#   python scripts/migrate_jobs_to_postgres.py
#   python scripts/migrate_rules_to_postgres.py


# =============================================================================
# 12. Docker Compose: ports, image, resource limits (ADR-0044, ADR-0046)
# =============================================================================
# The containers receive this whole file, so every section above applies.  The
# values below are read by `docker compose` itself.
#
# Where the host publishes the app.  127.0.0.1 = this machine only, like a host
# install; 0.0.0.0 exposes it with NO authentication.  To open it to others,
# prefer the `proxy` profile (TLS + password): it is the only thing meant to be
# published.  A value beyond loopback needs AUTH_MODE=local (or oidc, both) in this
# file: the container refuses to start with authentication off.
# CTI_BIND=127.0.0.1
# CTI_PORT=8000
# CTI_TLS_BIND=0.0.0.0
# CTI_TLS_PORT=8443

# Image name.
# CTI_IMAGE=ctiparsor:local
# Image name of the dev service (compose profile dev: scripts and tests).
# CTI_DEV_IMAGE=ctiparsor:dev
# false = no Chromium: a smaller image, but the URL-capture tab answers 503.
# CTI_INSTALL_CAPTURE=true
# Commit recorded in the image and in each bundle's run config.  Pass it on the
# command line rather than here, so it cannot go stale:
#   CTI_GIT_REV=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) docker compose build
# CTI_GIT_REV=
# Use a different env file for the containers only.
# CTI_ENV_FILE=.env

# CPU and memory per container.  The defaults are measured for one report at a
# time; set them to what the machine has.  The worker needs
# WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENT x 4.4 GB + 1 GB; past its limit the OOM killer takes
# the pipeline subprocess and the report fails, the worker itself survives.
# API: ~130 MB idle, 2.2 GB peak during a detection-corpus rebuild.
# CTI_APP_CPUS=2
# CTI_APP_MEMORY=3g
# CTI_WORKER_CPUS=4
# CTI_WORKER_MEMORY=6g
# CTI_DB_CPUS=1
# CTI_DB_MEMORY=512m
# CTI_PROXY_CPUS=0.5
# CTI_PROXY_MEMORY=128m
# CTI_BOOTSTRAP_CPUS=2
# CTI_BOOTSTRAP_MEMORY=4g
# Bundled `ollama` profile: 12g fits a 7B model in Q4; a 27B one needs ~18g
# and a GPU. CTI_OLLAMA_CPUS must not exceed the host's CPU count: Docker
# refuses to start the container otherwise.
# CTI_OLLAMA_CPUS=8
# CTI_OLLAMA_MEMORY=12g
# Context window of the bundled `ollama` service, in tokens.  The prompt and
# the answer must both fit.  A 7B model needs ~2 GB of memory per 16k tokens:
# raise CTI_OLLAMA_MEMORY with it.
# CTI_OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=16384
# Status file the `backup` service writes (ADR-0097 item 19), read by the app
# for GET /metrics.  The default is the path of the read-only mount in Compose;
# change it only if the volume is mounted elsewhere.
# BACKUP_STATUS_FILE=/app/backup-status/status.json
# Bearer token that lets a scraper read GET /metrics from another address
# (Prometheus on another host).  Empty: only loopback clients can read it.
# A secret: generate it with  openssl rand -hex 32  and keep it out of
# version control.  Serve /metrics only behind TLS (the `proxy` profile).
# Work item 14 adds the admin token next to it.
# METRICS_TOKEN=

# Scheduled backups: `docker compose --profile backup up -d backup` (docs/docker.md,
# Data and backups). BACKUP_TARGET is a host directory the backup writes to:
# another disk or an NFS share, not the Docker volume it protects. Unset, the
# rest of the stack starts, and the backup service refuses to back up.
# BACKUP_TARGET=/mnt/backups/ctiparsor
# Daily run time, UTC.
# BACKUP_TIME=02:30
# Backup pairs kept, the newest first. Each pair is one database dump and one
# archive of cti-state.
# BACKUP_KEEP=7


# =============================================================================
# 13. Logging
# =============================================================================
# DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# text | json
# LOG_FORMAT=text
# Also write logs to this file (empty = stdout only).  The two settings after
# it rotate that file: size in bytes, and how many old files to keep.
# LOG_FILE=
# MAX_LOG_SIZE=10485760
# LOG_BACKUP_COUNT=5
# Any value other than `development` skips the LLM provider check that is
# logged at startup (missing key, endpoint and model in use).
# ENV=development


# =============================================================================
# 14. Expert tuning: change only alongside a measurement
# =============================================================================
# Calibrated values.  tests/eval_pipeline.py is the harness for the TTP ones.
#
# Stage 2c cosine cutoffs, resolved per embedding model automatically (values
# shown are all-MiniLM-L6-v2's).  At or above HIGH: high confidence, wins over
# the LLM; at or above MEDIUM: a candidate; below: dropped.
# TTP_HIGH_THRESHOLD=0.62
# TTP_MEDIUM_THRESHOLD=0.48
# A 2nd technique matched to the same sentence is kept only within this margin
# of the best match (the main source of semantic false positives).
# TTP_TOP2_MARGIN=0.05
# Taxonomies the matcher may return.  ATT&CK only by default: CAPEC catalogues
# abstract patterns and mostly shadowed the ATT&CK technique a report
# describes (CAPEC-163 "Spear Phishing" beside T1566.001).  "all" restores it.
# TTP_SEMANTIC_DOMAINS=enterprise-attack,mobile-attack,ics-attack
# Require technical keywords before proposing a technique.
# TTP_KEYWORD_GATE=true
# Ignore mitigation text and table captions when matching.
# TTP_ADVISORY_GATE=true
# Rejoin lines broken by newlines before matching.
# TTP_UNWRAP_LINES=true
# Candidates kept per document.
# TTP_MAX_CANDIDATES=200
#
# TTP_MODE=select retrieval (ADR-0072; recall measured on AnnoCTR dev).
# Corpus: short (the legacy cache), description, procedure or both.
# TTP_RETRIEVAL_CORPUS=both
# Retriever: dense, bm25, minrank or rrf.
# TTP_RETRIEVAL_METHOD=rrf
# Techniques kept per passage, then per chunk (the selector's list; the LLM's
# own proposals are added on top, never cut).  10 / 40 put 86% of the gold
# techniques in their own chunk's list on AnnoCTR dev, 21 per chunk on average.
# TTP_CANDIDATES_PER_PASSAGE=10
# TTP_CANDIDATES_PER_CHUNK=40
# The keyword allow-list above, for retrieval.  It drops the passage of 36% of
# the annotated techniques, yet under the per-chunk cap recall is as good with
# it, and the lists shorter.
# TTP_RETRIEVAL_KEYWORD_GATE=true
# Chunks Stage 3 skips (no IoC, no known name) but with candidates: one
# selection call each.  On dev: 36 more calls for at most 4% more recall.
# TTP_SELECT_SKIPPED_CHUNKS=false
# A selection quote shorter than this many words is sent to review.
# TTP_SELECT_MIN_QUOTE_WORDS=3
# Output-token ceiling of the selection call, never above LLM_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS:
# a looping answer stops there and keeps its complete choices (ADR-0072).
# Unset: 4096 on vLLM with thinking off, where it was measured; any other
# provider keeps LLM_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS, since a model that reasons first can
# spend part of the budget before it answers.
# TTP_SELECT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=
# File of URL fragments: procedure examples citing one are not retrieved (the
# evaluation sets it so MITRE examples written from a scored report stay out).
# TTP_RETRIEVAL_EXCLUDE_CITED=

# Stage 2d / 2e batching: phrases per batch, and window size in characters.
# CYNER_BATCH_SIZE=4
# CYNER_CHUNK_CHARS=1600
# GLINER_BATCH_SIZE=4
# GLINER_CHUNK_CHARS=1600

# Tests and audit scripts only: skip spaCy, CyNER, GLiNER and the
# sentence-transformers models, so nothing is downloaded.  Never in production.
# SKIP_HEAVY_MODELS=
